    <!-- Main Content Overlapping Hero -->
    <main class="flex-1 max-w-4xl mx-auto w-full px-6 py-8 -mt-16 relative z-20 macos-glass mb-12">
        
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4"><span class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-white/10 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center gap-1"><svg class="w-3 h-3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 8v4l3 3m6-3a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg> 2 MIN READ</span></div><div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-8 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10 pb-6">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">Availability</h2>
            <div class="w-24 h-1 rounded-full opacity-80" style="background-color: #14b8a6"></div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="prose  max-w-none">
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>The Epidemic of Busyness</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">We live in a generation that wears "busy" as a badge of honor — as if an overflowing calendar proves our significance and a packed schedule validates our existence. But here's what's striking: Jesus never hurried. Not once.</p><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Mark 1:35 tells us that very early in the morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up, left the house, and went off to a solitary place to pray. The crowds were waiting. The sick needed healing.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">The disciples were searching for Him. And yet He withdrew. He created margin.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">If the Son of God — the One who held the weight of the world's salvation on His shoulders — needed solitude and space with the Father, how much more do we? Busyness is not the same as fruitfulness. You can be incredibly busy and completely empty at the same time. You can fill every hour and still miss the one thing that matters.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">The enemy doesn't always come with obvious temptation — sometimes he just keeps you so occupied with good things that you have no room left for God things.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>The Gift of Sabbath</h3>
                <div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Exodus 20:8-10 records the fourth commandment: "Remember the Sabbath day by keeping it holy. Six days you shall labor and do all your work, but the seventh day is a sabbath to the Lord your God." God didn't command rest because He was tired after creating the universe — He commanded it because He knows us. He knows we will run ourselves into the ground, mistake our productivity for our identity, and forget that we are human beings, not human doings.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Sabbath is a radical act of trust. It's looking at your to-do list, your deadlines, your responsibilities, and saying, "God, I believe the world won't fall apart if I stop. I believe You are sovereign over what I cannot finish." In a hustle culture that screams "grind harder, rest later," Sabbath is holy rebellion.</p><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">It's a weekly declaration that your worth is not measured by your output. It's anchored in the finished work of Christ, who said on the cross, "It is finished" (John 19:30) — not "keep going." Rest is not laziness; it's obedience. And it's one of the most countercultural things you can do as a young adult today.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Practical Steps to Create Margin</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Creating margin starts with an honest audit of your calendar — not just what's on it, but why it's on it. Are you saying yes out of obligation, fear of missing out, or people-pleasing? Or are you saying yes because the Holy Spirit led you there? Learn to say no to good things so you can say yes to God things. Not every opportunity is your assignment, and not every invitation deserves your energy.</p><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Build intentional weekly rhythms: one full day of rest where you unplug and recharge, daily time with God before the world gets your attention, and phone-free hours where you're actually present with the people in front of you. Protect your peace like you protect your schedule — because if you don't guard your margin, the world will gladly fill it for you. Psalm 46:10 says, "Be still, and know that I am God." Stillness isn't passive — it's a discipline.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">It takes more strength to stop than to keep going. Start small: block out one evening this week with nothing on the calendar. Sit with God.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Take a walk without earbuds. Let the silence do its holy work.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Available for God's Assignments</h3>
                <div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">In Luke 10:38-42, we find two sisters with two very different postures. Martha was busy serving — running around the kitchen, stressed about the meal, frustrated that Mary wasn't helping. Mary, on the other hand, sat at the feet of Jesus and listened.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">When Martha complained, Jesus responded with tender truth: "Martha, Martha, you are worried and upset about many things, but few things are needed — or indeed only one. Mary has chosen what is better, and it will not be taken away from her." This isn't a rebuke of hard work — it's a recalibration of priorities. When you have margin in your life, you have capacity for divine interruptions: the conversation God wants you to have with your coworker, the person He puts in your path at the grocery store, the ministry He's been calling you into but you've been too busy to hear.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Some of God's greatest assignments come disguised as interruptions. But you'll miss them if every minute is already spoken for. Availability is a spiritual posture.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">It says, "God, my time is Yours. My schedule is Yours. Use me today." The most impactful moments of your life will rarely be the ones you planned — they'll be the ones you had margin for.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        
